      ":question/id": "dce4995e-ba4f-5b1d-8ddd-05f9db6e21d3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{-2x-4y=6; 3x+y=7$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =7 \\\\ y & =7-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=7-3x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-4y & =6 \\\\ -2x-4(7-3x) & =6 \\\\ -2x-28+12x & =6 \\\\ 10x-28 & =6 \\\\ 10x & =34 \\\\ x & =\\frac{17}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{17}{5}$ into $y=7-3x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7-3(\\frac{17}{5}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{17}{5},\\,y=-\\frac{16}{5}.$",
